---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="About" description="Who writes EV Trip Reports, and why it is independent.">
  <section class="band band--tight">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h1 class="h1">About</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top:20px">EV Trip Reports is a set of stop-by-stop road trip guides for EV drivers, plus custom plans built around a specific car.</p>

      <h2>Who's behind it</h2>
      <p>I'm Zak Winnick. I work in EV charging infrastructure, and before that I spent 15 years in hospitality, mostly making sure guests didn't hit surprises. This site is the trip-planning version of that job.</p>
      <p>I started it because the information drivers need is scattered. Routing apps handle the math, but whether a site is open to your car, how many stalls it really has, and whether people waited there last weekend live on a dozen network locators and forum threads. These guides put that in one place, with a source on every fact.</p>

      <h2>Independence</h2>
      <p>No charging network pays for placement or coverage here, and none of them reviews a guide before it goes up. If a stop on a guide is a site I have a business connection to, that stop will say so.</p>
      <p>If we add affiliate links for hotels or gear, they will be marked where they appear. They will never decide which charger a guide recommends.</p>

      <h2>How the work gets done</h2>
      <p>AI tools do much of the research and upkeep: pulling station data, re-checking network locators, and collecting driver reports. The rules they follow are on the <a href="/methodology">methodology page</a>. Facts get a source and a check date, or they get marked as not yet verified.</p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>If you were at a stop recently and something doesn't match, we want to know. Use the <a href="/plans#request">request form</a> and put the stop and what you saw in the notes. A dedicated corrections address is coming.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
